Z||000000||rockcrawl||09-03-2001||02:03 PM||||Anybody have tips on removing the rear window from a Fiero? It seems like the regular methods used on a windshield will not work on the butyl that holds the rear window in.||||reg||5
Z||000001||Songman||09-03-2001||06:24 PM||||Scott just tried to take one out for me with no luck. He said he realized where it went wrong. Maybe he can tell you.... Maybe send him a PM.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Fleabitdog||09-03-2001||08:35 PM||||It is a real pain. Ski and I used a piece of wire with vise grips on each end and a sawing<BR>motion. It took awhile. The wire kept fraying. Definately a little easier with two people. Dave<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||skitime||09-03-2001||09:29 PM||||Hey Rockcrawl, Why you taking out a rear window?  BTW, I sent you a Personal Message (PM). Check on the main forum page.<p>[This message has been edited by skitime (edited 09-03-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||rockcrawl||09-03-2001||10:05 PM||||I know what you're thinkin Skitime! Nah, I'm not copyin'. I bought an '85 GT that needs a back window, and I have two parts cars that have (had) them. I spent about an hour trying to get one out and I gave up. I finally smashed them so I wouldn't have to worry about trying to get them out anymore! I also managed to break a good windshield trying to take it out. I finished off the cars with my Case 480 backhoe, it makes quick work of flattening any car. They are now loaded on the trailer ready to go for scrap. I just thought it would be nice to know how to get them out next time.<P>WARNING, the following images may be disturbing to some viewers. No rare or valuable parts were harmed in the making of these photos.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/DCP01426.jpg"> <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/DCP01425.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||skitime||09-03-2001||10:13 PM||||Hey Rockcrawl, I have two rear windows already out.  If you want one you are welcome to it for free.  Why don't you stop down sometime or I still want to come up and see your caddy swap and I could bring it along.  <P>------------------<BR><UL TYPE=SQUARE><BR><LI>Red 88 GT T-TOP<BR><LI>Red 86 GT<BR></UL> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/signature-1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Scott||09-04-2001||03:16 AM||||Hi rockcrawl,<BR>  BE CAREFUL!  They break easy!  I just tried to get one out this past weekend.  I used an old 1/2" chisel that I ground thinner to try to pry the window out enough to get a L-shaped knife in there.  Well after almost getting the clearance I needed to get the knife in, the chisel slipped, and the whole window was reduced to 1/4" rectangular pieces of glass held by the plastic laminate.  If I attempt it again, I'm going to use an Xacto knife and jewlers screw drivers to get a piece of guitar string through.  Then I'll saw out the window with the guitar string.  Good luck.<BR>Scott  ||||reg||1
Z||000007||cadaver||09-04-2001||11:22 PM||||So let me get this straight. You couldnt get them out so you smashed them. That makes sense. I have pulled a few back windows and a few windshields out and never broken one. Its not that hard.||||reg||1
Z||000008||rockcrawl||09-05-2001||07:14 AM||||No, it's not that hard but nobody else seems to think it's easy either. I have sucessfully removed them in the past. This time I did not have a helper to pull on the other end of the wire. That line about smashing them so I wouldn't have to worry about trying to get them out anymore was a joke. Sorry you didn't get it. This was a last minute attemt to remove any remaining good parts from the cars before they went to the crusher. I smashed them so I could stack two of them on the trailer and make one trip. <P>Anyway, I was just wondering if anyone had any neat tricks, but it looks like the two man wire method is the best. I was afraid the butyl would just rejoin after the wire passes by it. ||||reg||1
Z||000009||skitime||09-05-2001||01:16 PM||||I was able to remove mine by myself but it was a t-top. You could do it yourself in any Fiero if you have a longer wire. You just need to start in the middle of the glass and work to each side. I press a wire down through the seal from the inside to the outside using a putty knife. Get a hold of the wire from the outside.  I wrapped the ends around a vise grips to have sturdy handles. Slowly work the wire back and forth like a saw.  If you go too fast you will overheat the wire and it will break. Let the wire cool if it smokes.  They actually make a special wire for this and it is a lot easier with the correct wire. The correct wire has spiraled wire wrapped around the main wire. It is similar to a spiral hacksaw blade only flexable.  Try to get some from a windsheild shop.  Do not try to push the window out until you have cut the whole way around. <P>Rockcrawl do you want one of the rear window I have already removed?  You can have one for free.  ||||reg||1
Z||000010||rockcrawl||09-05-2001||03:34 PM||||Skitime, We'll definately have to hook up sometime. As soon as I get the Cadero registered and aligned I can drive it down there. Do those windows have defrosters? I have a good clear one, but I was trying to get one with defrost. I don't know if I really want Rr defrost anyway. I just though it would be a good idea to save them if I could get them out easily. Thank's for the tips too, I think the main problem was that I was not using the correct wire. I tried MIG welding wire and 1/16" steel wire rope. Both of them broke quickly.||||reg||1
Z||000011||skitime||09-05-2001||05:10 PM||||Rockcrawl, Definately!  Both I have are clear.  The wire you use really makes a difference.  In a pinch, like in a junkyard you can use hood cable but most smooth wire doesn't do much cutting. ||||reg||1
Z||000012||Patrick86GT||09-05-2001||09:19 PM||||I have been using guitar strings for years for the cutting of the seal. Specifically, Martin brand medium guage, steel, round wounds for acoustic guitar...in the set of six, only 4 are suitable for cutting the seal.  And you might have guessed, the "G" string is most desireable! <P>Also, it's a 2 person job.||||reg||1
Z||000013||skitime||09-05-2001||09:52 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Patrick86GT:<BR><B>The "G" string is most desireable! <P>Also, it's a 2 person job.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Truer words were never spoken.  hehe<BR>||||reg||1
